{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"The diagnostic resolved the anomaly that had blocked the read-index probe twice, and the probe now completes end to end. Reading the leader's own per-peer progress through RawNode.WithProgress showed matches {1:11, 3:11} — a quorum — while its commit stayed at 10, which pinned the cause on my harness rather than the target: my delivery helper never persisted Ready entries, so once the unstable log was marked stable the leader could read back neither its own entry nor its term and could never commit in its own term. I fixed the helper to act as a correct application (HardState and Entries written to the node's storage before Advance, messages delivered after that write) and the probe then observed the whole path: the request is queued with no read state while the leader lacks a commit in its term (index 15), the follower acknowledgement moves the commit to 11 with a quorum of matches, and the released read state carries read_index 11 with the original request context. I filed that as one fixed experiment for the read-index question with four comparisons — queueing, the commit premise, the release index, and the request-context identity — and validation accepted it with the execution requested. Incomplete: the receipt is pending, and this same harness deficiency explains the earlier aftermath probe's unexplained commit value, which I will note when that receipt is interpreted.\"}"}}
